// The handler-body shapes #2652 measured and left unfixed, in one fixture
// (#2659).  Each was a SILENT emitter defect — valid `.ddd`, output that either
// vanished or did not compile:
//
//   A. a handler touching NO aggregate — .NET's `emitExplicitHandlers` dropped
//      it (`if (!primaryAgg(h)) continue`), so `Echo` / `Sum` AND their routes
//      were absent from the .NET project while the other four emitted them.
//      They now file under the neutral `Application/Handlers/` folder the
//      extern handlers already use.  The same shape on PYTHON emitted a
//      handler module importing `NoopDomainEventDispatcher` with no repository
//      to construct — ruff F821's twin, F401, which is that leg's compile gate.
//   B. a declared find named `byId` called with an already-typed `Agg id` —
//      java renamed it to `getById` AND wrapped the argument, emitting
//      `getById(new OrderId(orderId))` where `orderId` is an `OrderId`
//      (`incompatible types` at javac), against a method the declared find is
//      not.  The declared method is now rendered verbatim, as everywhere else.
//   C. an OPTIONAL find bound in a handler body — the shared workflow statement
//      vocabulary has no null-handling arm, so the binding was dereferenced
//      unguarded (TS18047 / CS8602).  That shape is REFUSED at phase ⑦ now
//      (`loom.handler-load-nullable-unsupported`), mirroring the workflow twin,
//      so it cannot appear in a corpus fixture at all: its pin is
//      `test/ir/handler-nullable-load.test.ts`.
//
// Every find here returns an ARRAY on purpose.  A NON-optional SINGLE find
// (`find byCode(c: string): Order`) is a shape no corpus fixture reaches and
// the five backends do not agree on: node throws `AggregateNotFoundError`,
// .NET's `FirstAsync` throws, and java / python / elixir return null / None /
// nil — which mypy --strict catches on the python leg and nothing catches on
// the other two.  That divergence is a parity finding of its own, not this
// fixture's subject, and putting it here would gate this PR on settling it.
system HandlerTriad {
  subdomain D {
    context Sales {
      aggregate Order {
        code: string
        status: string
        // A self-reference, so a find can take an `Order id` and still filter
        // on a real column (the primary key is not a filterable field).
        replaces: Order id
        operation cancel() { status := "cancelled" }
      }
      repository Orders for Order {
        // B: named `byId` on purpose — the name java used to special-case,
        // renaming the call to `getById` and re-wrapping its argument.
        find byId(oid: Order id): Order[] where replaces == oid
      }

      // A, command leg: no repository, no aggregate — pure computation.
      commandHandler Echo(text: string): string {
        return text
      }
      // A, query leg: same, with two scalar params.
      queryHandler Sum(a: int, b: int): int {
        return a + b
      }
      // B: an `Agg id` path param flowing into the declared `byId` find.
      queryHandler CountReplacing(orderId: Order id): int {
        let matches = Orders.byId(orderId)
        return matches.count
      }
    }
  }
  api A from D {
    route POST "/echo/{text}" -> Sales.Echo
    route GET  "/sum/{a}/{b}" -> Sales.Sum
    route GET  "/orders/{orderId}/replacements" -> Sales.CountReplacing
  }
  storage pg { type: postgres }
  resource salesState { for: Sales, kind: state, use: pg }
  deployable d {
    platform: __PLATFORM__
    contexts: [Sales]
    dataSources: [salesState]
    serves: A
    port: 4000
  }
}
